#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120695} :activity/id #uuid "ad4d6410-a365-5f77-a4b1-3e2d134d5a3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638 :topic/id #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" :topic/math-academy-id 1864} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121042 :tutorial/content "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bb4f07e6-7c2f-5fe2-b96b-1bb37603981a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3208 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "55354f89-d587-5329-9788-b198ebb218a3" :step/math-academy-id 17513 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119631 :question/id #uuid "0a0ea798-5125-5d0e-a755-96bf9af5f543" :question/math-academy-id "e-4823"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4f1675-9146-5279-9b64-07a63a9bed99" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393563}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative To a Given Basis"} :step/id #uuid "b2734184-6a42-50f6-9a19-566236b06025" :step/math-academy-id 17514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119632 :question/id #uuid "6c7237cd-c655-575f-83ac-7a0891c336b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4824"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "841f498b-f737-5086-8f79-44f1a143bebb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391903}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "94aa1ebf-d415-58aa-a0dc-17e8201c1669" :step/math-academy-id 17515 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119633 :question/id #uuid "90f07e38-405a-5f79-8501-0b4dfb8fa4c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4825"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6a6ea8-6100-5224-b75f-b87c037fafbb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345167}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing More Than Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "34b75a42-9505-5418-a27f-2b9aade2bc07" :step/math-academy-id 17516 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121066 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a set of vectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that the set $\\mathcal{S}$ is linearly independent since the vector equation $x_1\\mathbf{e}_1+x_2\\mathbf{e}_2+x_3\\mathbf{e}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ leads to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution. \n\nAlso, notice that any vector in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{e}_1,$ $\\mathbf{e}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{e}_3.$ For instance,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn particular, the equation above tells us that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthat is to say, the numbers $\\color{red}-2,$ $\\color{red}0,$ $\\color{red}5$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nThe basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is somewhat special, since the coordinates of a vector relative to this basis coincide with the components of the vector. We refer to $\\mathcal{S}$ as the **standard basis**. Remember that in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3$ we even have special notations for the vectors of the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd96e0b70173adf8983feb4975c351ab3e417c34ed4a6a944bc391e33db4c8e9.png)\n\nIn general, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "54e7c1fd-c7bf-5c77-bcc5-eb8d93a11582" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3969 :tutorial/title "The Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9053925c-8744-5312-b890-f5e4a1a8d2e8" :step/math-academy-id 23574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119674 :question/id #uuid "a79126f2-e26c-5929-93d8-825d85b9a438" :question/math-academy-id "e-6282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2926764-dfbc-5e4e-a6b0-db24a7298a64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345152}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "79095f38-ffd3-565b-a534-f136c7646422" :step/math-academy-id 23612 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120697}}] :activity/title "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120054}]}
